  "instructions": "First test actual image access: Read ONLY the first chronological page of the first bound sequence before reading guides or renderer source. If that page is stripped or unseen, retain the literal error and its exact page identity, return partial coverage and no verdict, and stop image/guide traversal. This tests transport only; it supplies no observation of unseen pages. If visible, record its actual note, then read every required full guide and the complete ordered sequence. Read the bound current inputs and your brief. Load cited source evidence as needed. Do not copy production history. Return one consolidated verdict. Never infer audio access from text. Read the complete story_contract in the bound board, including all scenes, transitions and source limits. Mandatory completion capacity preserves the rubric and actual verdicts; no resource event approves a film. Read the bound craft guides in full. Use them to explain editorial decisions and inspect actual comprehension; they supply no approval or extra scoring criteria. Inspect each measured spoken clause against its executable subject and performed action in the actual film. Read board.narration_picture; reject wrong or absent pictures even when captions or provider prose repeat the words. Review every chronological image page for both options before the director rationale. These images establish only their sampled pixels, with no direct playback or listening. Return one consolidated comparison/selection and exact-phone review as JSON in your handback; the director saves it unchanged. Copy the supplied mechanical bindings exactly, then supply your own verdict and all required timed observations. Unproven motion stays unresolved. After the complete chronological viewing, consolidate the handback. Recheck only named uncertain intervals; return unresolved findings instead of repeated viewing to pursue a pass. Do not run Bash, write files, invent hashes, or infer approval from structural measurements.After each image Read, provide a brief public image description as a factual JSON record with page, objects, actions and text in ordinary assistant text BEFORE another Read. Record only visible objects, visible actions and literal on-screen text; use null for unknowns. Put quality findings and brief public explanations only in the final review JSON. Preserve these factual records for the coverage report.  A prelaunch image-tool delivery receipt records the actual returned payload, not whether you saw it. Record visibility when the new Read returns. An older image becoming a request-limit placeholder later is not evidence that its original Read failed; preserve the earlier observation and keep later retention loss distinct from initial access failure. A newly unseen payload still requires immediate partial coverage and its actual error. No delivery receipt supplies a creative grade. Image delivery is sequential: issue ONE image Read, wait for it, and record the page filename, first/last frame and a concrete visible observation before the next image Read. Never send parallel image Reads or a bulk image batch. Earlier images can leave the host window; retain your actual textual observations and consolidate from those notes without bulk rereading. If a page is stripped or unseen, return partial immediately with its exact identity; do not infer pixels from source code, timestamps or earlier stills. In the final handback also return a separate coverage object with schema dispatch_claude_image_coverage/1, packet_sha256, and sequences in packet order. Each sequence has index_sha256 and pages in index order; each page has file, first_frame, last_frame, received (true only when actually seen), and observed (at least thirty characters describing its actual visible content). Preserve every required guide, timed observation and honest verdict.",
